<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- The app may now reach the network, but it still has no way to reach
         the city. There is exactly one URL constant in the source (the relay,
         net/RelayClient.kt), no city hostname anywhere, and
         network_security_config.xml permits cleartext to localhost only.
         Decision 0002 says the apps never post to the city directly; this
         permission exists to talk to our own relay and nothing else. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <!-- CameraX pulls androidx.media3, whose manifest declares this. Nothing
         here reads connectivity, so it stays stripped. -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
        Declared explicitly, and required. The camera is the entry point: the
        app opens on it and there is no path that starts with a form
        (AGENTS.md), so a device without one cannot use this at all and should
        not be offered it.

        Android lint's PermissionImpliesUnsupportedChromeOsHardware wants
        required="false" here, on the assumption that excluding camera-less
        Chrome OS devices is an accident. For this app it is the specification.
        The check is disabled in build.gradle.kts with that reasoning rather
        than satisfied with a declaration that would be false.

        GPS is a different matter and is NOT required: testing indoors found
        that the network provider is usually the one holding a usable fix, and
        a device without GPS can still place a report (see DeviceFix).
    -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.camera"
        android:required="true" />
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.location.gps"
        android:required="false" />

    <!--
        Both, and it has to be both. Since Android 12 the system dialog for a
        FINE request offers the user an "Approximate" button, and an app that
        declared only FINE is simply denied when they press it. Declaring COARSE
        as well is what makes that press mean "coarse granted" instead of
        nothing, which for this app is the difference between a report placed on
        the wrong street and no report at all.

        Coarse is not good enough to find a bin, so the app still asks for FINE
        and says so; what this buys is a usable answer rather than a refusal
        when someone grants less than we asked for.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />

    <application
        android:name=".BorgarlandApplication"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:label="Borgarland POC"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
